𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟰𝟮/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗥𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗨𝗽𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗰𝗸 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗙𝗶𝘅 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You updated a Kubernetes Deployment, but the rolling update is stuck. Some old Pods are still running, and the new Pods are not becoming Ready. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't delete the old Pods immediately. First, I'd identify why the new version isn't becoming healthy.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check rollout status

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>

Check whether the Deployment has reached its desired number of available replicas.

2️⃣ Inspect Pods

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for "ImagePullBackOff", "CrashLoopBackOff", "Pending", or readiness probe failures.

3️⃣ Check ReplicaSets

kubectl get rs -n <namespace>

Confirm whether the new ReplicaSet was created and whether it can create healthy Pods.

4️⃣ Review application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

Look for startup errors, missing configuration, failed dependencies, or application crashes.

5️⃣ Check rollout events and configuration

kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Investigate resource shortages, image issues, probes, ConfigMaps, Secrets, and recent changes.

6️⃣ Recover safely

If the new release is causing the issue and rollback is approved:

kubectl rollout undo deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Verify the rollback and confirm that the application is healthy.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

A rolling update can get stuck when new Pods fail to become Ready, preventing Kubernetes from progressing with the rollout.

Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods → Readiness → Rollout Status

Understand the failure before taking action, and always verify application health after recovery.
